%[text] # Build Manipulator Robot Using Kinematic DH Parameters
%[text] Use the Denavit-Hartenberg (DH) parameters of the Puma560® manipulator robot to incrementally build a rigid body tree robot model. Specify the relative DH parameters for each joint as you attach them. Visualize the robot frames, and interact with the final model.
%[text] The DH parameters define the geometry of how each rigid body attaches to its parent via a joint. The parameters follow a four transformation convention:
%[text] - $A${"editStyle":"visual"} — Length of the common normal line between the two *z*-axes, which is perpendicular to both axes
%[text] - $\\alpha${"editStyle":"visual"} — Angle of rotation for the common normal
%[text] - $d${"editStyle":"visual"} — Offset along the *z*-axis in the normal direction, from parent to child
%[text] - $\\theta${"editStyle":"visual"} — Angle of rotation for the *x*-axis along the previous *z*-axis \
%[text] Specify the parameters for the Puma560 robot [\[1\]](internal:M_A476C8DD) as a matrix.
dhparams = [0   	pi/2	0   	0;
            0.4318	0       0       0
            0.0203	-pi/2	0.15005	0;
            0   	pi/2	0.4318	0;
            0       -pi/2	0   	0;
            0       0       0       0];
%%
%[text] Create a rigid body tree object.
robot = rigidBodyTree;
%%
%[text] Create a cell array for the rigid body object, and another for the joint objects. Iterate through the DH parameters performing this process:
%[text] 1. Create a `rigidBody` object with a unique name.
%[text] 2. Create and name a revolute `rigidBodyJoint` object.
%[text] 3. Use `setFixedTransform` to specify the body-to-body transformation of the joint using DH parameters. The function ignores the final element of the DH parameters, `theta`, because the angle of the body is dependent on the joint position.
%[text] 4. Use `addBody` to attach the body to the rigid body tree. \
bodies = cell(6,1);
joints = cell(6,1);
for i = 1:6
    bodies{i} = rigidBody(['body' num2str(i)]);
    joints{i} = rigidBodyJoint(['jnt' num2str(i)],"revolute");
    setFixedTransform(joints{i},dhparams(i,:),"dh");
    bodies{i}.Joint = joints{i};
    if i == 1 % Add first body to base
        addBody(robot,bodies{i},"base")
    else % Add current body to previous body by name
        addBody(robot,bodies{i},bodies{i-1}.Name)
    end
end

%%
%[text] Verify that your robot has been built properly by using the `showdetails` or `show` function. The `showdetails` function lists all the bodies of the robot in the MATLAB® command window. The `show` function displays the robot with a specified configuration (home by default).
showdetails(robot) %[output:53e941fc]
figure(Name="PUMA Robot Model")
show(robot);
%%
%[text] ## Interact with Robot Model
%[text] Visualize the robot model to confirm its dimensions by using the `interactiveRigidBodyTree` object. 
figure(Name="Interactive GUI")
gui = interactiveRigidBodyTree(robot,MarkerScaleFactor=0.5); %[output:1d8ae92d]
%[text] Click and drag the marker in the interactive GUI to reposition the end effector. The GUI uses inverse kinematics to solve for the joint positions that achieve the best possible match to the specified end-effector position. Right-click a specific body frame to set it as the target marker body, or to change the control method for setting specific joint positions.
%[text] ![](text:image:52ed)
%%
%[text] ## Next Steps
%[text] Now that you have built your model in MATLAB®, these are some possible next steps.
%[text] - Perform [inverse kinematics](docid:robotics_doccenter#mw_533ce698-c4bd-430c-8cb9-add59bd96640) to get joint configurations based on desired end-effector positions. Specify robot constraints in addition to those of the model parameters, including aiming constraints, Cartesian bounds, and pose targets.
%[text] - [Trajectory Generation and Following](docid:robotics_doccenter#mw_d5333cca-a634-4d69-9ff5-1e99b99ab0e4), based on waypoints and other parameters, with trapezoidal velocity profiles, B-splines, or polynomial trajectories.
%[text] - Perform [Manipulator Motion Planning](docid:robotics_doccenter#mw_87184aa9-6b29-46a6-a396-aab126d8274f) utilizing your robot models and a rapidly-exploring random tree (RRT) path planner.
%[text] - Use [Collision Detection](docid:robotics_doccenter#mw_d93555e3-d6a3-4974-9088-680f8c5845f3) with obstacles in your environment to ensure safe and effective motion for your robot. \
%%
